{"reference":"Micah 3","book":"Micah","chapter":3,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Micah 3 addresses the people who hold public and religious authority: rulers, judges, priests, prophets, seers, and diviners. The chapter’s harsh images of stripping skin and breaking bones portray their conduct as violent exploitation, while its later accusations identify justice distorted for reward, teaching for hire, and divination for money. Their confidence that the presence of Jehovah guarantees safety is set against the announcement that Zion and Jerusalem will be devastated.","verse_refs":["Micah 3:1-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_micah_003_d7fbc0ffd14e25353c0c3e2f"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions of Setting and Structure","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Later tradition preserves Micah 3:12 as a saying of Micah of Moresheth in the days of Hezekiah, and it was invoked in a later dispute about prophetic judgment. The chapter itself gives sustained attention to land, housing, courts, leadership, and the tension between city and countryside, but it does not explicitly identify Micah’s own social class or political role; reconstructions of those details remain uncertain.","verse_refs":["Micah 3:1-12","Micah 3:12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_micah_003_b2532563a6fd62356a0e69bb"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"The opening call to “Hear” may mark the beginning of a major new movement in the book. One influential outline sees comparable calls near 1:2, 3:1, and 6:1 as dividing Micah into three cycles, though this reading does not settle every change of speaker or possible seam in the book.","verse_refs":["Micah 3:1"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_micah_003_845bec8270bd535822199a17"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Within the Book of Micah","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Micah as a whole brings together varied forms of speech, including lament, accusation, woe, judgment, salvation oracle, disputation, confession, prayer, and praise. In one influential reading, its final form repeatedly alternates judgment and hope across the movements of chapters 1–2, 3–5, and 6–7. That wider pattern helps place this chapter’s concentrated judgment alongside the book’s broader range of voices.","verse_refs":["Micah 1:1-16","Micah 2:1-13","Micah 4:1-13","Micah 5:1-15","Micah 6:1-16","Micah 7:1-20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_micah_003_34fe5d1cd1cd52813df2a9d5"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}